{"records":[{"id":"9w4g54bv43","title":"Genome-wide screens reveal that resveratrol induces replicative stress in human cells","description":"Resveratrol is a natural product associated with wide-ranging effects in animal and cellular models including lifespan extension. To identify the genetic target of resveratrol in human cells, we conducted genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screens to pinpoint genes that confer sensitivity or resistance to resveratrol. An extensive network of DNA damage response and replicative stress genes exhibited genetic interactions with resveratrol and its analog pterostilbene. These genetic profiles showed similarity to the response to hydroxyurea, an inhibitor of ribonucleotide reductase that causes replicative stress. Resveratrol, pterostilbene and hydroxyurea caused similar depletion of nucleotide pools, inhibition of replication fork progression and induction of replicative stress. The ability of resveratrol to inhibit cell proliferation and S phase transit was independent of the histone deacetylase Sirtuin 1, which has been implicated in lifespan extension by resveratrol. These results establish that a primary impact of resveratrol on human cell proliferation is the induction of low-level replicative stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-04T14:56:19.488Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9w4g54bv43","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9w4g54bv43"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yahya","last_name":"Benslimane","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thierry","last_name":"Bertomeu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mary","last_name":"McQuaid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Sánchez-Osuna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Papadopoli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daina","last_name":"Avizonis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariana","last_name":"De Sa Tavares Russo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Huard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Topisirovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hugo","last_name":"Wurtele","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mike","last_name":"Tyers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lea","last_name":"Harrington","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","High-Throughput Screening","High-Throughput Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sj8yykm6bn","title":"Raw data for Sati et al., 2020","description":"These datasets are contain Beta gal assay images, DAPI stained nuclei and 3D FISH images from oncogene and replicative senescent cells. The Betagal assay was performed to demonstrate the senescent condition. The DAPI stained nuclei images are from different siRNA treated OIS samples. The FISH images display the presence of SHADs in SAHFs.\n\nThe Summary of work:\nWhile transcriptional and epigenetic changes associated with senescence are well studied, the role of the extensive senescence-associated 3D genome reorganization remains elusive. Here, we have generated genome wide chromatin interaction maps, epigenetic, replication-timing, whole genome bisulfite sequencing and gene expression profiles from cells entering replicative senescence (RS) or upon oncogene induced senescence (OIS). We identify Senescence Associated Heterochromatin Domains (SAHDs). Differential intra vs inter SAHD interactions lead to the formation of senescence associated heterochromatin foci (SAHFs) in OIS but not in RS. This OIS-specific configuration brings active genes located in genomic regions adjacent to SAHDs in close spatial proximity and favours their expression. Finally, screening of factors for SAHF induction revealed DNMT1 as a novel component that induces SAHFs by promoting HMGA2 expression. Upon DNMT1 depletion, OIS cells transition to a 3D genome conformation akin to that of cells in replicative senescence. These data show how multi-omics and imaging can identify critical features of RS and OIS and discover new determinants of acute senescence and SAHF formation.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-13T18:08:00.967Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sj8yykm6bn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sj8yykm6bn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Satish","last_name":"Sati","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Giacomo","last_name":"Cavalli","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chromosome","Chromatin","Cellular Senescence","Genome","Epigenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pm5v6pwkkg","title":"Genome-wide screens implicate loss of cullin ring ligase 3 in persistent proliferation and genome instability in TP53 deficient cells","description":"TP53 deficiency is the most common alteration in cancer, however, alone is typically insufficient to drive tumorigenesis. To identify genes promoting tumorigenesis in combination with TP53 deficiency, we performed genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens coupled with proliferation and transformation assays in isogenic cell lines. Loss of several known tumor suppressor genes enhanced cellular proliferation and transformation. Surprisingly, loss neddylation pathway genes, promoted uncontrolled proliferation exclusively in TP53-deficient cells. CUL3 coupled with TP53 loss results in an oncogenic transcriptional program governed by the NFκB, AP-1 and TGF-β pathways. This program maintains persistent cellular proliferation, induces partial epithelial to mesenchymal transition, increases DNA damage, genomic instability, and the rate chromosomal rearrangements. Our findings reveal CUL3 loss as a key event stimulating persistent proliferation in TP53 deficient cells. These findings may be clinically relevant, since TP53-CUL3 deficient cells are highly sensitive to ATM inhibition, exposing a vulnerability that could be exploited for cancer treatment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T17:31:10.704Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pm5v6pwkkg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pm5v6pwkkg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexandros","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2bbvdfrrct","title":"Regulation of replicative histone RNA metabolism by the histone chaperone ASF1","description":"In S phase, duplicating and assembling the whole genome into chromatin requires upregulation of replicative histone gene expression. Here, we explored how histone chaperones control histone production in human cells to ensure a proper link with chromatin assembly. Depletion of the ASF1 chaperone specifically decreases the pool of replicative histones both at the protein and RNA levels. The decrease in their overall expression, revealed by total RNA-seq, contrasted with the increase in nascent/newly synthesized RNAs observed by 4sU-labeled RNA-seq. Further inspection of replicative histone RNAs showed a 3'-end processing defect with an increase of pre-mRNAs/unprocessed transcripts likely targeted to degradation. Collectively these data argue for a production defect of replicative histone RNAs in ASF1-depleted cells. We discuss how this regulation of replicative histone RNA metabolism by ASF1 as a “chaperone checkpoint” fine-tunes the histone dosage to avoid unbalanced situations deleterious for cell survival.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-10T06:06:46.84Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2bbvdfrrct","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2bbvdfrrct"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dominique","last_name":"Ray-Gallet","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Geneviève","last_name":"Almouzni","orcid":"0000-0001-5570-0723"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Histone","Chromatin"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gttn89vbpn","title":"Chromatin-associated YTHDC1 coordinates heat-induced reprograming of gene expression","description":"Summary of the paper:\nHeat stress (HS) induces a cellular response leading to profound changes in gene expression. Here, we show that human YTHDC1, a reader of N6-methyladenosine (m6A) RNA modification, mostly associates to the chromatin fraction, and that HS induces a redistribution of YTHDC1 across the genome, including to heat-induced heat shock protein (HSP) genes. YTHDC1 binding to m6A-modified HSP transcripts co-transcriptionally promotes expression of HSPs. In parallel, hundreds of the genes enriched in YTHDC1 during HS have their transcripts undergoing YTHDC1- and m6A-dependent intron retention. Later, YTHDC1 concentrates within nuclear stress bodies (nSBs) where it binds to m6A-modified SATIII non-coding RNAs (ncRNAs), produced in a HSF1-dependent manner upon HS. These findings reveal that YTHDC1 plays a central role in a chromatin-associated m6A-based reprograming of gene expression during heat stress. Furthermore, they support the model where the temporary sequestration of YTHDC1 within nSBs subsequently calibrates the timing of this YTHDC1-dependent gene expression reprograming. \nCell Reports 2022","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-19T20:35:25.476Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gttn89vbpn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gttn89vbpn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kalina","last_name":"Timcheva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Solenne","last_name":"Dufour","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Leila","last_name":"Touat-Todeschini","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Callum","last_name":"Burnard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marie-Christine","last_name":"Carpentier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Florent","last_name":"Chuffart","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rémy","last_name":"MERRET","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marion","last_name":"Helsmoortel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sabrina","last_name":"Ferré","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aude","last_name":"Grezy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yohann","last_name":"Couté","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sophie","last_name":"Rousseaux","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Saadi","last_name":"Khochbin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Claire","last_name":"VOURC'H","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Cecile","last_name":"Antonelli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rosemary","last_name":"Kiernan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daphne","last_name":"Seigneurin-Berny","orcid":null},{"first_name":"André","last_name":"Verdel","orcid":"0000-0001-6048-3794"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene Expression","Human Cell","RNA Methylation","Cellular Stress Response"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"czj8xvj9sf","title":"Rad5 recruits error-prone DNA polymerases for mutagenic repair of ssDNA gaps on undamaged templates. Gallo et al.","description":"Gallo et al. explore how cells repair ssDNA that arises from exogenous and endogenous DNA replication stress. They reveal that recruitment of error-prone polymerases to stressed replication forks results in mutagenic repair of ssDNA. These findings point to an expanded role of error-prone polymerases in replicating the eukaryotic genome. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-12-11T14:22:55.084Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/czj8xvj9sf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/czj8xvj9sf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Gallo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","Yeast","Translesion DNA Polymerase"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":109245,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":132,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":109245,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":108776,"restricted":53,"embargoed":416},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1898},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":570},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":428},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":367},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":360},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":358},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":338},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":294},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":238},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":192}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":106711},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1887},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":172},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":113},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":66},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":63},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":47},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":29},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":29},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":454},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":386},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":340},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":278},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":255}]}}